{
  "requirement": "In Markdown, escaped roles and role-looking text in inline code, fenced code, or raw HTML MUST remain literal and MUST NOT count as references. A `{req}` directive inside a fenced code example and a legacy inline `{req}` role MUST NOT define a requirement.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: false.** The implementation looks complete, but no test covers raw HTML, one of the three protected contexts the requirement names. The implementation covers every obligation, and the {impl} marking is complete.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `reference_roles` (src/lib.rs:670) skips any `{` that `is_escaped` or `is_protected` flags. `is_escaped` (src/lib.rs:752) uses odd-backslash parity, so `\\{role}` stays literal.\n- `protected_ranges` (src/lib.rs:730) uses pulldown-cmark offsets. It protects `Tag::CodeBlock` (fenced and indented), `Event::Code` (inline code), `Event::Html` (HTML blocks) and `Event::InlineHtml`. That covers all three contexts the requirement names.\n- `definition_blocks` (src/lib.rs:508) only recognises colon-fence `:::{req}` openers and closers. It rejects any opener that overlaps a code-block range via `overlaps_protected` (src/lib.rs:663). A fenced `:::{req}` example therefore defines nothing, and a legacy inline `{req}`​`` `name` `` role never defines anything.\n- All helpers carry {impl} markers, and the callers (evidence collection, `replace_references`, terms, index) go through these functions. I found no other code that implements part of the requirement without a marker.\n\n**Tests**\n- `code_examples_and_legacy_roles_remain_literal` (src/lib.rs:972) covers inline code, a fenced `:::{req}` block and a legacy `{req}` role. It also checks that a real definition still renders.\n- `markdown_examples_and_escaped_references_stay_literal` (src/lib.rs:1056) covers inline code, a fenced block and a backslash-escaped `{impl}` role. It checks that no badge is produced.\n- `colon_directives_render_markdown_bodies_and_legacy_roles_stay_literal` (src/lib.rs:1081) covers a legacy inline role.\n- `references_in_requirement_bodies_are_scanned_as_markdown` (src/lib.rs:1096) shows a fenced reference is not counted by `reference_roles`.\n- `source_references_are_covered_by_evidence_inputs` (src/evidence.rs:1610) shows inline and fenced code in a Markdown file do not count as references during validation.\n\n**Gap**\n- The requirement names \"raw HTML\", and the implementation handles it through the `Html` and `InlineHtml` events. No test I found exercises it. A search of src and tests for HTML fixtures found none that feed role-looking text inside raw HTML to `reference_roles` or the preprocessor.\n- Because of that, a regression in that branch of `protected_ranges` would go undetected.\n- There is no FIXME or other acknowledgement of this gap in the sources, so FIXME does not apply.\n\nAdding a test with a role inside an HTML block or inline HTML, such as `<span>{impl}`x`</span>` or an HTML comment, would close the gap.",
